From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Skia] Blur larger shadows at half resolution sooner

Skia blurs in one full-resolution pass an axis up to sigma 4, a shader of
up to 14 texture reads a pixel (72 and 84 instructions on the Mali-450,
whose fragment processors sample slowly), and downscales first only above
that. A CSS box-shadow, on almost every element of a modern page, is
mostly in that range. Downscale above sigma 2: such a shadow is blurred at
half resolution with half the taps, a fraction of the reads, and is as
soft to the eye.

---
--- a/Source/ThirdParty/skia/src/core/SkBlurEngine.h
+++ b/Source/ThirdParty/skia/src/core/SkBlurEngine.h
@@ -191,7 +191,9 @@
     // used, but never updated after the linear filtering optimization reduced the number of
     // sample() calls required. Keep it at 4 for now to better isolate performance changes due to
     // switching to a runtime effect and constant loop structure.
-    static constexpr float kMaxLinearSigma = 4.f; // -> radius = 27 -> linear kernel width = 28
+    // Halved for GPUs that sample slowly, as the Mali-450: a larger blur is downscaled first, so a
+    // CSS shadow takes a fraction of the texture reads, blurred at half resolution.
+    static constexpr float kMaxLinearSigma = 2.f;
     // NOTE: There is no defined kMaxBlurSigma for direct 2D blurs since it is entirely dependent on
     // the ratio between the two axes' sigmas, but generally it will be small on the order of a
     // 5x5 kernel.
